-- Known, user-reported (NOT independently tested) production metadata:
-- conversations.id TEXT; messages.id UUID; messages.conversation_id TEXT.
-- conversations.user_id and messages.user_id nullable; legacy NULL rows exist.
-- Separate user/parent FKs exist; composite ownership constraint not demonstrated.
-- Integration UNIQUE(user_id, provider) exists; retain it for Batch 3 upserts.
--
-- Intended invariant for future app-owned records:
-- messages(conversation_id, user_id) must reference the same owner's parent.
-- A composite FK requires a suitable existing or explicitly reviewed unique
-- parent key (id, user_id). Do not invent a new index, constraint name or drop
-- an existing FK without its full definition and dependencies.
--
-- Required canonical evidence before choosing final DDL:
-- * complete columns/defaults/nullability, PKs/UNIQUE/CHECKs and index definitions;
-- * every relevant FK, MATCH, ON DELETE/UPDATE, deferrability and validation state;
-- * triggers/functions, table ownership, role/grant/policy details and PG version.
--
-- Preserve existing delete/update behavior exactly. A second FK can interact
-- with the old FK's actions; CASCADE, RESTRICT, NO ACTION, SET NULL and SET DEFAULT
-- are not interchangeable. Composite SET NULL may change additional columns.
-- Therefore no ON DELETE/UPDATE rule is specified or changed in this draft.
--
-- Legacy rows stay in place: no ownership guesses, UPDATE, DELETE or backfill.
-- Consider NOT VALID FK/CHECK staging only after reviewing existing constraints.
-- MATCH SIMPLE skips a NULL key component; it alone cannot enforce a non-NULL
-- owner for future writes. A NOT VALID CHECK can block updates of legacy rows;
-- that consequence requires review. UNIQUE and NOT NULL need separate planning.
--
-- Separate HTTP ownership checks cannot replace this invariant or an atomic
-- parent lock/check plus related writes. Future transaction/RPC work requires
-- separate reviewed SQL; no SECURITY DEFINER function is introduced here.

ROLLBACK;
